/-! Quasi-isomorphism of the scalar-extended augmented model.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebraization, Tags 099I and 099H.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-flat-ringed-cech-base-change-quasi-iso).

After the scalar extension and augmentation have been constructed, restriction to
finite generating slices identifies the local terms with coefficientwise extended
localization complexes. Flatness and the quotient isomorphism give local
quasi-isomorphisms, and exactness plus local vanishing of kernels and cokernels
globalizes this to `QuasiIso u`.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
universe u w
open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace CategoryTheory

/-- The scalar-extended augmented map is a quasi-isomorphism.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebraization, Tags 099I and 099H.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-flat-ringed-cech-base-change-quasi-iso).

Proof: restrict to a slice on which the finite-type ideal is generated by a finite list.
Exact restriction transports the augmented local description. Flatness restricts to the
slice, and the fraction maps identify the scalar-extended local terms, differentials and
augmentations with the coefficientwise extended localization Cech complex. Apply the flat
extended Cech comparison on each slice. These slices cover every object, so the kernels and
cokernels of the homology maps vanish locally and hence globally; therefore `u` is a
quasi-isomorphism. The theorem is isolated because this local-to-global comparison is the
only nontrivial remaining step in the parent construction.
-/
/- STATEMENT-DISPUTED: The augmentation equation alone does not force a quasi-isomorphism.
A formal biproduct argument shows this universal conclusion would force every target
homology object to be zero: apply it to `T` and to `T ⊞ T` with the first injection.
The source requires the specific scalar-extended Cech model and its comparison map,
which are absent from this declaration. -/
-- STATEMENT-DISPUTED: augmentation compatibility alone is insufficient.
theorem flat_ringedCech_baseChange_data_quasiIso
    {C : Type u} [SmallCategory C] {J : GrothendieckTopology C}
    {R B : Sheaf J CommRingCat.{u}} (φ : R ⟶ B) (I : RingedSiteIdeal R)
    [HasDerivedCategory.{w} (SheafOfModules.{u} (siteModuleRings R))]
    [∀ U : C, HasDerivedCategory.{w}
      (SheafOfModules.{u} (siteModuleRings (R.over U)))]
    (hI : SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType.{u, u, u} I.module)
    (hflat : SheafOfModules.IsFlat
      ((SheafOfModules.restrictScalars.{u}
        ((sheafCompose J (forget₂ CommRingCat RingCat)).map φ)).obj
          (SheafOfModules.unit (siteModuleRings B))))
    (hquot : I.IsQuotientIso φ)
    (K : ringedSiteComplex R) (ε : K ⟶ ringedSiteRingComplex R)
    (hK : HasLocalExtendedCechDescription R I.module I.inclusion K ε) :
    let r := SheafOfModules.restrictScalars.{u}
      ((sheafCompose J (forget₂ CommRingCat RingCat)).map φ)
    let rc := r.mapHomologicalComplex (.up ℤ)
    let σ : ringedSiteRingComplex R ⟶ rc.obj (ringedSiteRingComplex B) :=
      (HomologicalComplex.single _ (.up ℤ) 0).map
          (SheafOfModules.LocalizationCech.unitMap φ) ≫
        (HomologicalComplex.singleMapHomologicalComplex r (.up ℤ) 0).inv.app
          (SheafOfModules.unit (siteModuleRings B))
    ∀ (L : ringedSiteComplex B) (δ : L ⟶ ringedSiteRingComplex B)
      (u : K ⟶ rc.obj L), u ≫ rc.map δ = ε ≫ σ → QuasiIso u := by
  sorry

end CategoryTheory
